## 2. Prerequisites (one-off)

- Docker, `zstd`, `curl` and `python3` on this machine.
- An SSH alias `K3s-server` in `~/.ssh/config` with key-based login (`BatchMode` must work).
- Your user on the server can run `kubectl` without sudo (kubeconfig in `~/.kube`).
- Your server password, for the `sudo` import in phase 5.
- Optional: a Plane API token in `PLANE_TOKEN`, for the MCP check in phase 7.

---

## 3. Phase-by-phase

### Phase 0: preflight checks

Checks tools, SSH, kubectl, disk space and that the working tree is clean. Stops at the first
problem.

```bash
cd "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"
for cmd in docker zstd curl python3 ssh git; do
  command -v "$cmd" >/dev/null || { echo "missing: $cmd"; false; }
done
docker info >/dev/null && echo "docker: ok"
ssh -o BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=15 K3s-server 'kubectl -n plane get deploy >/dev/null && echo "ssh + kubectl: ok"; df -h ~ | tail -1'
git status --short -- apps packages | grep -v '^??' && echo "WARNING: uncommitted changes in apps/ or packages/ will be baked into the images" || echo "git: tracked files clean"
echo "HEAD: $(git log -1 --format='%h %s')"
```

### Phase 0b: clean the Docker cache

Start each release from an empty build cache, so stale layers can't end up in the images.
This removes only the build cache and untagged images. Volumes and the images used by the
local `platform` infra containers stay. The builds that follow are cold (see the timing above).

```bash
docker system df
docker builder prune -af | tail -1
docker image prune -f | tail -1
```

### Phase 1: plan the release

First see what changed since the last release. The last release's commit is read from
the top row of the release log (section 6), so keep that log up to date (phase 8).

```bash
cd "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"
LAST="$(awk -F'`' '/^\| `release\./{print $4; exit}' deployments/local_dev/deploy-to-K3s)"
echo "last release commit: $LAST"; git log --oneline "$LAST"..HEAD
git diff --name-only "$LAST"..HEAD -- apps packages | cut -d/ -f1-2 | sort | uniq -c
for p in $(git diff --name-only "$LAST"..HEAD -- packages | cut -d/ -f2 | sort -u); do
  printf '  @plane/%-10s used by:' "$p"
  for a in web space admin live; do grep -q "\"@plane/$p\"" "apps/$a/package.json" && printf ' %s' "$a"; done; echo
done
echo "new migrations:"; git diff --name-only --diff-filter=A "$LAST"..HEAD -- 'apps/api/plane/db/migrations/*.py' | sed 's/^/  /'
```

Any new migration means `backend` ships, and phase 6 runs the migrator. Read each one now:
see the note before the phase 6 script.

Then set **which components** to ship (space-separated, see the matrix). This block fixes the
tag, defines the component mapping, and saves the plan to `~/.cyb-release`.

```bash
cd "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"
COMPONENTS="backend web"                       # <- edit: any of backend web space admin live proxy
T="release.$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H-%M-%SZ).1"
COMMIT="$(git rev-parse --short HEAD)"
LAST="$(awk -F'`' '/^\| `release\./{print $4; exit}' deployments/local_dev/deploy-to-K3s)"

cat > ~/.cyb-release <<EOF
export T="$T" COMPONENTS="$COMPONENTS" COMMIT="$COMMIT" LAST="$LAST" REPO="$PWD"
dockerfile_of() { case "\$1" in backend) echo apps/api/Dockerfile.api;; web) echo apps/web/Dockerfile.web;;
  space) echo apps/space/Dockerfile.space;; admin) echo apps/admin/Dockerfile.admin;;
  live) echo apps/live/Dockerfile.live;; proxy) echo apps/proxy/Dockerfile.ce;; *) return 1;; esac; }
context_of() { case "\$1" in backend) echo apps/api;; proxy) echo apps/proxy;; web|space|admin|live) echo .;; *) return 1;; esac; }
images_of() { local c; for c in \$COMPONENTS; do printf 'cybernetics-project-%s:%s ' "\$c" "\$T"; done; }
EOF
source ~/.cyb-release
for c in $COMPONENTS; do dockerfile_of "$c" >/dev/null || { echo "unknown component: $c"; false; }; done
echo "release $T  commit $COMMIT  components: $COMPONENTS"
echo "currently deployed:"
ssh -o BatchMode=yes K3s-server 'kubectl -n plane get deploy -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,IMAGE:.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image --no-headers'
```

### Phase 1b: check for new env vars and config

Lists the env vars the new code reads, and the keys the cluster already has. Only key
names are printed, never secret values.

```bash
source ~/.cyb-release && cd "$REPO"
echo "new env reads (backend):"
git diff "$LAST"..HEAD -- apps/api ':!apps/api/plane/tests' | grep '^+' \
  | grep -oE '(os\.environ\.get|os\.getenv)\("[A-Z0-9_]+"[^)]*\)?|os\.environ\["[A-Z0-9_]+"\]' | sort -u | sed 's/^/  /'
echo "new build-time vars (frontends):"
git diff "$LAST"..HEAD -- apps packages | grep '^+' | grep -oE 'VITE_[A-Z0-9_]+' | sort -u | sed 's/^/  /'
ssh -o BatchMode=yes K3s-server 'echo "plane-config keys:"; kubectl -n plane get cm plane-config -o go-template="{{range \$k,\$v := .data}}  {{\$k}}{{\"\n\"}}{{end}}"
  echo "plane-secret keys:"; kubectl -n plane get secret plane-secret -o go-template="{{range \$k,\$v := .data}}  {{\$k}}{{\"\n\"}}{{end}}"'
```

How to read the result:

- **A var with a default in the code** (like `os.environ.get("TWO_FACTOR_CODE_TTL", 600)`)
  needs no change. Add it to the cluster only if you want a value other than the default.
- **A var without a default** that isn't in either list has to be added before the
  rollout. Non-secret values go in `apps/plane/configmap.yaml` on the server, which is
  part of the kustomization. Secrets go in `apps/plane/secret.yaml`, which is gitignored:
  apply it with `kubectl apply -f apps/plane/secret.yaml`. Pods don't pick up configmap or
  secret changes until they restart, and phase 6 only restarts the deployments in the
  release. For any other deployment, run `kubectl -n plane rollout restart deploy/<name>`.
- **`VITE_*` vars are baked in at build time.** Set them before phase 2, or the frontend
  image has to be rebuilt.
- **Instance settings live in the database, not in env vars.** Production runs with
  `SKIP_ENV_VAR=True`, so SMTP, sign-in methods and other god-mode settings come from
  `/god-mode/`, and env values for them are ignored. A feature that sends email (2FA codes,
  invites, password resets) needs SMTP configured there. Use **Send test email** in god-mode.

### Phase 2: build the images

Builds every planned component. A failed build stops the loop. Logs are written to
`/tmp/cyb-build-<component>.log`.

```bash
source ~/.cyb-release && cd "$REPO"
for c in $COMPONENTS; do
  echo "== building $c -> cybernetics-project-$c:$T"
  if ! docker build -f "$(dockerfile_of "$c")" -t "cybernetics-project-$c:$T" \
      --label "org.opencontainers.image.revision=$COMMIT" "$(context_of "$c")" \
      > "/tmp/cyb-build-$c.log" 2>&1; then
    echo "   BUILD FAILED: $c (full log: /tmp/cyb-build-$c.log)"; tail -20 "/tmp/cyb-build-$c.log"
    break
  fi
  echo "   ok"
done
docker images --format '{{.Repository}}:{{.Tag}}  {{.Size}}' | grep -F "$T"
```

### Phase 3: smoke-test the images locally

These checks catch a broken image before it reaches the server. The backend test boots
Django with the test settings and builds the MCP server. It needs no database.

```bash
source ~/.cyb-release
for c in $COMPONENTS; do
  case "$c" in
    backend)
      docker run --rm --entrypoint sh -e DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE=plane.settings.test \
        -e SECRET_KEY=smoke -e REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379 "cybernetics-project-backend:$T" -c '
        python manage.py check >/dev/null && echo "   backend: manage.py check ok"
        python -c "
import asyncio, django; django.setup()
from django.conf import settings; settings.MCP_READ_ONLY=False; settings.MCP_TOOLSETS=[]
from plane.mcp.server import build_server
print(\"   backend: django ok, MCP tools:\", len(asyncio.run(build_server().list_tools())))"' ;;
    web|admin)
      docker run --rm --entrypoint sh "cybernetics-project-$c:$T" -c \
        'test -s /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html && echo "   '"$c"': index.html ok, $(find /usr/share/nginx/html -name "*.js" | wc -l) js files"' ;;
    space)  # Node server, not nginx: boot it and request /spaces/
      docker run -d --rm --name cyb-space-smoke -p 127.0.0.1:39300:3000 "cybernetics-project-space:$T" >/dev/null
      for i in $(seq 30); do code=$(curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' http://127.0.0.1:39300/spaces/); [ "$code" != 000 ] && break; sleep 1; done
      echo "   space: GET /spaces/ -> $code (expect 200)"; docker stop cyb-space-smoke >/dev/null ;;
    *)
      docker image inspect "cybernetics-project-$c:$T" >/dev/null && echo "   $c: image present" ;;
  esac
done
```

To check that a particular change made it into an image, grep for something new. The
images use BusyBox `grep`, which has no `--include`:

- Web bundle: `docker run --rm --entrypoint sh cybernetics-project-web:$T -c 'grep -rlF "<new string>" /usr/share/nginx/html | head -1'`
- Backend migration or template: `docker run --rm --entrypoint sh cybernetics-project-backend:$T -c 'ls plane/db/migrations | tail -3; ls templates/emails'`

### Phase 4: ship to the server

Streams the images, zstd-compressed, straight into a tarball on the server, then
checks the archive. Backend and web compress to about 160 MB. With space and admin
added, it's about 430 MB, roughly a minute over the LAN.

```bash
source ~/.cyb-release
set -o pipefail
docker save $(images_of) | zstd -T0 -3 -q | ssh -o BatchMode=yes K3s-server "cat > ~/cyb-$T.tar.zst" \
  && ssh -o BatchMode=yes K3s-server "zstd -t -q ~/cyb-$T.tar.zst && ls -lh ~/cyb-$T.tar.zst && echo integrity-ok"
set +o pipefail
```

### Phase 5: import into containerd (you, needs your password)

This step needs `sudo`, so you run it yourself. Print the exact command:

```bash
source ~/.cyb-release
echo "ssh -t K3s-server 'zstd -dc ~/cyb-$T.tar.zst | sudo k3s ctr -n k8s.io images import -'"
```

Run the printed command in a terminal. From inside Claude Code, prefix it with `! `. It
prints one `docker.io/library/cybernetics project … saved` line per image. Ignore the
containerd `DEPRECATION` warning about `configs`.

Then confirm the node has the images. This uses kubectl, so no sudo. The kubelet only
refreshes the node's image list every so often, so this retries for up to a minute:

```bash
source ~/.cyb-release
for i in $(seq 6); do
  FOUND="$(ssh -o BatchMode=yes K3s-server "kubectl get nodes -o jsonpath='{range .items[*].status.images[*]}{.names}{\"\n\"}{end}'" \
    | grep -o "cybernetics-project-[a-z]*:$T" | sort -u)"
  [ "$(grep -c . <<<"$FOUND")" -ge "$(wc -w <<<"$COMPONENTS")" ] && break; sleep 10
done
echo "$FOUND"   # expect one line per component in $COMPONENTS
```

The node's image list holds at most **50 images**, largest first. With many old images on
the node, a newly imported image may never show up, even though the import worked.
Confirm with `ssh -t K3s-server "sudo k3s crictl images | grep $T"`, and keep the node
tidy (phase 8).

### Phase 6: roll out

This block runs entirely on the server. In order, it:

1. **Records the release.** Copies the current manifests to `~/plane-releases/<tag>/` (the
   rollback point) and writes a `release.txt` with the old and new images.
2. **Bumps the tags.** Rewrites the image tag of each component in its manifest(s), and
   fails if any manifest wasn't updated. The sed expression matches `cybernetics-project-<c>:<anything>`,
   so a hand-edited ad-hoc name (like the old `plane-web:6-oct-2026`) has to be renamed
   first.
3. **Checks the node.** Every new image must be on the node, otherwise it aborts and
   restores the manifests. It retries for a minute while the node's image list catches
   up. If `crictl` shows an image but the node list never does (the 50-image limit, see
   phase 5), set `SKIP_NODE_CHECK=1`.
4. **Shows the diff.** Runs `kubectl diff -k` and prints only the changed `image:` lines, plus a
   count of any *other* changed lines. Other changes mean the live cluster and the repo
   have drifted; read them before continuing.
5. **Migrates.** If `backend` is in the release, deletes and re-creates the `migrator` Job and
   waits for it to complete (up to 15 minutes). A failed migration stops the rollout before
   any app pod is replaced.
6. **Rolls out.** Runs `kubectl apply -k apps/plane/` and waits for each affected deployment.

Set `DRY_RUN=1` to stop after step 4. A dry run restores the manifests and leaves the
cluster unchanged. Always do a dry run first. The diff should show only `image:` lines:
one per component, plus `worker`, `beat-worker` and `migrator` for backend.

**Before the real run, read every new migration** (phase 1 lists them). Additive
operations (`AddField`, `CreateModel`, `AddIndex`) are safe to apply while the old pods
are still running. Anything that drops, renames or rewrites data needs a plan for rolling
back, so read [Rollback](#4-rollback) first. To check what production has applied:
`ssh K3s-server 'kubectl -n plane exec deploy/api -- python manage.py showmigrations db | tail -3'`.

```bash
source ~/.cyb-release
DRY_RUN=1                                   # <- set to 0 for the real rollout
ssh -o BatchMode=yes K3s-server "DRY_RUN=$DRY_RUN SKIP_NODE_CHECK=${SKIP_NODE_CHECK:-0} bash -s -- '$T' $COMPONENTS" <<'REMOTE'
set -euo pipefail
T="$1"; shift; COMPONENTS="$*"
cd ~/dev-ops/k3s-deployment
APP=apps/plane
REL=~/plane-releases/$T

manifests_of() { case "$1" in backend) echo "backend.yaml migrator-job.yaml";; web|space|admin) echo frontend.yaml;;
                 live) echo realtime.yaml;; proxy) echo proxy.yaml;; *) return 1;; esac; }
deploys_of()   { case "$1" in backend) echo "api worker beat-worker";; *) echo "$1";; esac; }
restore()      { cp "$REL"/*.yaml "$APP"/; echo "   manifests restored from $REL"; }

echo "== 1. recording release in $REL"
# A finished rollout leaves its rollback copy here; re-using the tag would overwrite it
[ -e "$REL" ] && { echo "   $REL already exists: $T was already rolled out. Plan a new tag (phase 1)"; exit 1; }
mkdir -p "$REL"
cp "$APP"/*.yaml "$REL"/
{ echo "release: $T"; echo "date: $(date -u +%FT%TZ)"; echo "components: $COMPONENTS"
  echo "previous images:"; grep -h "image: cybernetics-project-" "$APP"/*.yaml | sort -u | sed 's/^ */  /'; } > "$REL/release.txt"

echo "== 2. bumping image tags"
for c in $COMPONENTS; do
  for f in $(manifests_of "$c"); do
    sed -i -E "s#(image: cybernetics-project-$c:)[^[:space:]\"']+#\1$T#" "$APP/$f"
    grep -q "image: cybernetics-project-$c:$T" "$APP/$f" || { echo "   tag not set for $c in $f"; restore; exit 1; }
    echo "   $c -> $f"
  done
done

echo "== 3. checking images on the node"
on_node() { kubectl get nodes -o jsonpath='{range .items[*].status.images[*]}{.names}{"\n"}{end}' | grep -q "cybernetics-project-$1:$T"; }
for c in $COMPONENTS; do
  for i in 1 2 3 4 5 6; do on_node "$c" && break; sleep 10; done
  if on_node "$c"; then echo "   present: cybernetics-project-$c:$T"
  elif [ "${SKIP_NODE_CHECK:-0}" = "1" ]; then echo "   not listed, continuing (SKIP_NODE_CHECK=1): cybernetics-project-$c:$T"
  else echo "   MISSING on node: cybernetics-project-$c:$T (run the phase 5 import)"; restore; exit 1; fi
done

echo "== 4. kubectl diff"
DIFF="$(kubectl diff -k "$APP"/ 2>&1 || true)"
grep -E '^[-+] +image:' <<<"$DIFF" || echo "   (no image changes?)"
OTHER="$( { grep -E '^[-+][^-+]' <<<"$DIFF" || true; } \
  | { grep -v -E 'image:|generation:|resourceVersion|managedFields|time:|^[-+] +f:' || true; } | wc -l)"
if [ "$OTHER" -gt 0 ]; then
  echo "   NOTE: $OTHER other changed line(s): the cluster has drifted from the repo; review with: kubectl diff -k $APP/"
fi

if [ "${DRY_RUN:-1}" = "1" ]; then
  restore; rm -rf "$REL"; echo "== DRY RUN complete, nothing applied"; exit 0
fi

if grep -qw backend <<<"$COMPONENTS"; then
  echo "== 5. running migrations"
  kubectl delete job migrator -n plane --ignore-not-found
  kubectl apply -f "$APP/migrator-job.yaml"
  if ! kubectl wait --for=condition=complete job/migrator -n plane --timeout=900s; then
    kubectl -n plane logs job/migrator --tail=40 || true
    echo "   MIGRATION FAILED: app pods were NOT replaced. Manifests still carry the new tag;"
    echo "   restore them with: cp $REL/*.yaml ~/dev-ops/k3s-deployment/$APP/"
    exit 1
  fi
  kubectl -n plane logs job/migrator --tail=5
fi

echo "== 6. applying and rolling out"
kubectl apply -k "$APP"/ | grep -v unchanged || true
for c in $COMPONENTS; do
  for d in $(deploys_of "$c"); do kubectl -n plane rollout status "deploy/$d" --timeout=300s; done
done
kubectl -n plane get deploy -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,READY:.status.readyReplicas,IMAGE:.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image
echo "== rollout complete; rollback point: $REL"
REMOTE
```

### Phase 7: verify

```bash
source ~/.cyb-release
URL=https://projects.avarile.com
echo "web:       $(curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' $URL/)"                  # 200
echo "instances: $(curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' $URL/api/instances/)"    # 200
ssh -o BatchMode=yes K3s-server 'kubectl -n plane get pods --no-headers | grep -v -E "Running|Completed" || echo "pods: all Running"'
ssh -o BatchMode=yes K3s-server 'kubectl -n plane logs deploy/api --since=5m | grep -i -E "traceback|exception|\"levelname\": \"ERROR\"" | head -10 || true'
ssh -o BatchMode=yes K3s-server 'kubectl -n plane get pods --no-headers -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,RESTARTS:.status.containerStatuses[0].restartCount,CREATED:.metadata.creationTimestamp'
ssh -o BatchMode=yes K3s-server 'kubectl -n plane logs deploy/worker --since=10m | grep -c "\. plane\.bgtasks\." | sed "s/^/worker: tasks registered: /"'

# MCP endpoint (only when PLANE_TOKEN is set), expects the server's tool count
if [ -n "${PLANE_TOKEN:-}" ]; then
  curl -s -X POST "$URL/api/mcp" -H "Authorization: Bearer $PLANE_TOKEN" \
    -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -H 'Accept: application/json, text/event-stream' \
    -H 'MCP-Protocol-Version: 2025-06-18' -d '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"tools/list"}' \
    | python3 -c 'import json,sys; print("mcp tools:", len(json.load(sys.stdin)["result"]["tools"]))'
fi
```

The pods in the release should have a fresh `CREATED` time and 0 restarts.

**Probe new routes.** POST to each new endpoint without logging in, for example
`curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' -X POST https://projects.avarile.com/auth/<new-route>/`.
A 400, 401 or 403 response means the route is deployed. So does Django's CSRF page, which
comes back as HTTP 200 with the title "CSRF Verification Failed". A 404 means the route
isn't deployed or isn't registered.

**Then use the feature itself in the browser.** Email (2FA codes, invites, password resets)
is sent by the Celery **worker**, not the API, so keep
`ssh K3s-server 'kubectl -n plane logs deploy/worker -f'` open while you trigger it. You
should see the task received, then `Email sent successfully.`

If anything looks wrong, go to [Rollback](#4-rollback). The rollback takes about a minute.

### Phase 8: clean up

```bash
source ~/.cyb-release
ssh -o BatchMode=yes K3s-server "rm -f ~/cyb-$T.tar.zst && echo 'server tarball removed'"
# Record the release in the server's kustomize repo (commit the bumped tags):
ssh -o BatchMode=yes K3s-server "cd ~/dev-ops/k3s-deployment && git add apps/plane && git commit -q -m 'plane: release $T ($COMMIT)' && git log -1 --oneline"
```

Then add a row for the release to the release log (section 6), newest on top, and commit
it. Phase 1 of the next release reads its commit from that row.

**Old images.** After every release, keep the current and the previous release of each
component and remove the rest. The previous release is the rollback point, and its images
must stay on the node. Besides saving disk space, this keeps the node under the 50-image
limit of its image list (phase 5).

On this machine, keep only this release's images, and clear the build cache and logs. A
rollback uses the images on the node, so nothing older is needed here:

```bash
source ~/.cyb-release
docker images --format '{{.Repository}}:{{.Tag}}' | grep '^cybernetics-project-' | grep -vF ":$T" | xargs -r docker rmi
docker builder prune -f | tail -1; rm -f /tmp/cyb-build-*.log
```

On the node, the next block *prints* a removal command covering every tag older than the
newest two of each image. Tags sort chronologically. Check the list against the deployed
images (`kubectl -n plane get deploy -o wide`), then run the printed command yourself with
`! `, because it needs sudo:

```bash
ssh -o BatchMode=yes K3s-server "kubectl get nodes -o jsonpath='{range .items[*].status.images[*]}{.names}{\"\n\"}{end}'" \
  | grep -oE 'cybernetics-project-[a-z]+:release\.[^",]+' | sort -u \
  | awk -F: '{n[$1]++; t[$1,n[$1]]=$0} END {for (r in n) for (i=1;i<=n[r]-2;i++) print "docker.io/library/" t[r,i]}' \
  | xargs -r echo "ssh -t K3s-server sudo k3s crictl rmi"     # no output: nothing to remove
```

Never remove an image that a Deployment still references, or that you may roll back to.
The node list can lag for a minute after a removal. `crictl`'s `Deleted:` lines are what
count.

